You reconcile two independent policy-rule extractions against the authoritative policy text.

Authoritative policy text:
<policy_text>
{policy_text}
</policy_text>

ODRL endpoint extraction:
<odrl_extraction>
{odrl_result}
</odrl_extraction>

Independent AI extraction:
<independent_ai_extraction>
{llm_result}
</independent_ai_extraction>

Deterministic comparison:
<comparison>
{comparison}
</comparison>

Produce the best source-grounded set of atomic rules. Neither extraction is ground truth.

Rules:
- Resolve disagreements only from the authoritative policy text.
- Preserve every supported permission, obligation, and prohibition.
- Split coordinated actions into separate atomic rules.
- Every output rule must contain exactly one action. Never join actions with a semicolon, comma, "and", "or", or "then".
- Use only "permission", "obligation", "prohibition", or an empty string for rule_type.
- Normalize actions to concise verb phrases without modal verbs.
- Do not discard a supported assignee, asset, constraint, or purpose merely because the two extractors format it differently.
- Attach "once" to download and "30 days" to retention when those limits are expressed by the policy; do not move a constraint to a different action.
- Keep unsupported fields empty; never invent missing actors, assets, constraints, or purposes.
- source_text must be an exact quotation from the policy.
- Return only JSON, without an explanation or Markdown.

Return exactly:
{{
  "rules": [
    {{
      "source_text": "",
      "assignee": "",
      "action": "",
      "asset": "",
      "rule_type": "",
      "constraint": "",
      "purpose": ""
    }}
  ]
}}
